\chapter{Formal Verification Framework}
\label{ch:verification}

To guarantee microkernel-grade security alongside bare-metal performance,
Telix employs a layered formal verification strategy. Rather than viewing the
operating system in isolation, the verification architecture establishes a
formal linkage between **hardware state dynamics** (ISA state transitions,
page table walk invariants, relaxed memory semantics) and **kernel memory safety**
(type preservation, encapsulation, and data-race freedom).

\section{Hardware State Modeling and Architectural Integrity}

The kernel's primary operational role is to drive state transitions in underlying
hardware peripherals, MMU translation tables, and CPU register files without
corrupting state invariants. Model choices depend on target ISA semantics and memory architectures:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Sail Specification Language}] \footcite{armstrong19}:
    Acts as the foundational ISA semantic model for RISC-V and ARMv8. Sail models
    provide executable instruction specifications and automatically synthesize
    machine-checked definitions for theorem provers (Coq/Isabelle) and SMT solvers.

    \item[\textbf{Katamaran}] \footcite{katamaran23}:
    A semi-automatic separation logic verifier built directly on Sail. Katamaran
    is ideal if Telix leverages capability-based hardware models (e.g., CHERI/Morello),
    as it verifies universal contracts directly against hardware specification state transitions.

    \item[\textbf{VRM (Verification on Relaxed Memory)}] \footcite{sosp21vrm}:
    A specialized framework for verifying multiprocessor OS kernel code managing shared
    page tables under relaxed memory architectures (such as ARM or RISC-V weakly-ordered memory models).
    VRM enables verifying concurrent page table updates under sequential consistency assumptions,
    automatically lifting the proofs to relaxed hardware operational models (\texttt{rmem}) \footcite{pulte18rmem}.
\end{description}

\section{Kernel Memory Access and Race-Freedom Verification}

At the language layer, Telix relies on Rust's strong static type system for safe code.
However, kernel boot sequences, MMU configuration, and atomic lockfree synchronization
require \texttt{unsafe} abstractions. We evaluate three primary verification frameworks for these boundaries:

